---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../../lib/site.ts";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const updated = "24 October 2026";
const githubPrivacy = "https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Privacy | Volt Mixer"
    description="Volt Mixer's privacy statement: no accounts, cookies, analytics or tracking; your mix stays on your computer.">
    <article class="privacy">
        <p class="hint">
            <a href={base}>← Volt Mixer</a>
        </p>
        <h1>Privacy statement</h1>
        <p class="hint">Last updated {updated}. Applies to the Volt Mixer website and the Volt Mixer desktop app.</p>

        <h2>In short</h2>
        <p>
            Volt Mixer has no accounts, cookies, analytics, advertising or tracking, and doesn't send anything about you
            or your Volt to anyone. Everything it remembers stays on your computer.
        </p>

        <h2>What stays on your computer</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Your mix (fader, pan and cue levels, channel names and icons), your view and theme choices, and which
                channels show in the tray. The website keeps these in your browser's storage for this site; the desktop
                app keeps them in two small files in your user data folder (its settings, and your mix), and the theme
                in its own storage.
            </li>
            <li>
                The mix is filed under your Volt's serial number, which the Volt reports when it connects, so two Volts
                keep separate mixes. The serial number isn't sent anywhere.
            </li>
            <li>
                On a phone, or any browser that can't reach a Volt, a mix link you open is kept in that browser so you
                can carry it to a computer, until you choose Forget it.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            To remove it all, clear this site's data in your browser, or uninstall the desktop app and delete its
            folder.
        </p>

        <h2>Your Volt</h2>
        <p>
            Volt Mixer talks to the Volt over USB (WebHID) on your computer, and only after you allow it (in the desktop
            app, only the Volt is allowed). It reads the Volt's settings and sends the changes you make. Nothing it
            reads leaves your computer.
        </p>

        <h2>Mix links</h2>
        <p>
            A mix link carries your mix in the part of the address after the #, which browsers don't send to any server.
            Anyone you give the link to can see the mix and your channel names.
        </p>

        <h2>The website's host</h2>
        <p>
            The website is served by GitHub Pages. Like any web server, GitHub receives your IP address and browser
            details when the page loads, as described in <a href={githubPrivacy}>GitHub's privacy statement</a>. Volt
            Mixer itself adds nothing to that.
        </p>

        <h2>The desktop app</h2>
        <p>
            The app only goes online when you choose Settings › Desktop app › Updates › Check, which asks GitHub for the
            latest release, and when you open a link (that opens in your browser). It has no telemetry and never updates
            itself.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you turn on Phone remote, the app answers phones that have scanned its QR code, only on the network you
            turned it on in. It sends nothing to the internet.
        </p>

        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <p>
            Volt Mixer is open source, so everything above can be checked in <a href={site.repositoryUrl}>its code</a>.
            Ask anything by opening an issue on <a href={`${site.repositoryUrl}/issues`}>GitHub</a>. Changes to this
            statement are recorded in the repository's history.
        </p>
    </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    .privacy {
        display: grid;
        gap: var(--space-between-content);
        max-inline-size: 42rem;
        margin-inline: auto;
        padding: var(--space-padding-page);
    }

    h2 {
        margin-block-start: var(--space-between-content);
    }

    ul {
        display: grid;
        gap: var(--space-between-text);
        padding-inline-start: 1.25rem;
    }

    a {
        color: var(--color-text-accent);
        text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
    }
</style>
